A||N||9||87GTBro||Help me...  I can't drive my fiero and it's driving me crazy!!!||||8||
Z||000000||87GTBro||12-30-1999||02:44 AM||||I made a big mistake.  I bought my '86 GT for $1750 cash from a private owner and never got the original title from him.  <P>I KNOW I KNOW (I'm stupid!!!)  First time I have bought a car for me.<P>The bank he financed the car through in '95 changed owners and the title was most likely lost in the changeover.  I am not sure the previous owner is sincere in his efforts to get the original title from the bank.  I could shoot myself twice over for giving him the money.<P>But now I have a beautiful shiny red '86 GT in my front yard that laughs at me every time I see it because I can't drive it because I don't have the title and registration and inspection stickers!!!<P>Pardon my naivete, but is there a way to get around the title mumbo jumbo or do I have to keep kicking myself waiting for the title to finally arive?  I want to drive my car!!!||||reg||8
Z||000001||Monkeyman||12-30-1999||02:59 AM||||Apply for a duplicate title.  If you can show a bill of sale (you did get that, didn't you?!) to the license bureau and explain the circumstances, they should be willing and able to help you out.  Best of luck.||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||12-30-1999||03:43 PM||||If the previous owner's bank had a lien on the title, you can still get the paper work straighten around with a duplicate title. The previous owner has to apply for it and sign it over to you. If there is a lien, the bank can release the lien on the duplicate title. Offer to pay for all the paper work expenses and offer to drive the guy to the courthouse to get the duplicate title, that way he is less likely to just blow it off. Keep hounding him until he's sick of you and will do it just to get you off his back..||||reg||1
Z||000003||kong||12-30-1999||05:13 PM||||Hey 85Bro, If by some chance you can't do any of the suggestions listed so far. Go to <A HREF="http://users.aol.com/fordmoval/broadway.html" TARGET=_blank>http://users.aol.com/fordmoval/broadway.html</A>   I've used them twice with cars with no titles. It'll cost you around $150 or $200, but for a last resort it worked for me. The instructions are on their site. Good luck!||||reg||14
Z||000004||kong||12-30-1999||05:19 PM||||Damn!!! Just tried the URL and it didn't go through, If it doesn't for you try their phone# Broadway title co. 1-800-464-3222. It was still in business a year ago when I last used them.||||reg||1
Z||000005||87GTBro||12-30-1999||11:21 PM||||There is a lien against the vehicle from a bank that no longer exists.  The car was paid off, probably around the same time that this bank was purchased by a larger one.  But the lien was never released.<BR>I talked to the previous owner's wife today and they seem to be trying their best to get the title.  <BR>I have the duplicate title in my posession.  But the tax office won't take that for the transfer.  They want the original.<BR>I am almost positive that the original title was lost in the bank changeover and I don't know if this new bank can release a lien that is listed from another bank.  I am hoping they can.  I want to drive my car!||||reg||1
Z||000006||Monkeyman||12-31-1999||04:33 AM||||The tax office (and everyone else) have to take the duplicate title.  The duplicate voids the original, so the original (wherever it is) is worthless now.  If you were to find the original and take it to the license bureau, they would tell you that it was no good because there is a dup.  (As always, I know this from experience! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">)||||reg||1
Z||000007||Sage||12-31-1999||09:19 PM||||Have gone thru this with two different cars.  If you have the duplicate title, you have the title number.  With the title number, you can apply for a lost title with the title office or dmv in the state the title was issued.  It cost me about $5.00 for mine.  A phone call will get you an application for lost title: fill it out, send it back in and they will issue a new title.  At least that's how it worked for me. When you get the new title, you have to take it to the title holder, which should be the party you bought the car from, and have them sign it over to you. Then you can have it transfered, and you are done.  Good luck||||reg||1
Z||000008||Cooter||12-31-1999||09:42 PM||||You could also apply for an abandoned vehicle title, since it is now abandoned on your property.||||reg||1
Z||000009||87GTBro||01-01-2000||03:50 AM||||I think we've got it all sorted out.  If all goes well, the title will be in my name by next weekend.  Thank God!!!  I'll get to drive my baby!!!||||reg||12
